\details{
\strong{Matched draws, not independent ones.} koma's stochastic forecasts are
famously unstable call-to-call: two identical \code{forecast()} calls on the
same fit gave \code{de_gdp} means differing by an order of magnitude more than
any real scenario effect (see \code{\link[=conditional_forecast_check]{conditional_forecast_check()}}'s roxygen).
Differencing two \emph{independent} stochastic forecasts would inherit that
noise on both legs and swamp any real spillover.

The fix is common random numbers. \code{koma::forecast()}'s posterior
coefficients for draw \code{i} are read deterministically from
\code{fit$estimates[[eq]]$beta_jw[[i]]} etc. -- no RNG involved. The only
randomness is the future-innovation draw \code{z_matrix <- matrix(rnorm(...))}
inside \code{forecast_draw()} (koma source, \code{forecast_sem.R}), drawn \strong{before}
the restriction branch and consumed identically whether or not
restrictions are present -- restrictions only \emph{add} a conditional
correction on top. So calling \code{set.seed(seed)} immediately before each
top-level \code{forecast()} call reproduces the identical innovation draw for
draw index \code{i} in both calls, and the difference at that index isolates
the model's response to the restriction. \strong{Verified}: for a variable
structurally unrelated to the shocked one, the matched-seed diff was
\emph{exactly} zero at every draw; an unmatched-seed diff on the same pair had
sd around 1 -- nine orders of magnitude larger, for a quantity that should
be zero. See \verb{data/cache/spillovers/} scratch notes for the check.

\strong{Alignment guard.} koma drops any draw whose forecast call errors
(\code{purrr::safely}), \emph{by subsetting the list} -- so \code{fc$forecasts[[i]]}
after a drop no longer corresponds to posterior draw \code{i}. If baseline and
scenario drop a different set of draws, pairing by list position would
silently mismatch. This aborts rather than risk that if the two calls
return different draw counts.

\strong{Exogenous-variable scenarios} (e.g. an oil-price shock) cannot use
\code{restrictions} at all -- koma's restriction mechanism only targets
\code{sys_eq$endogenous_variables}, since it conditions the \emph{reduced-form
innovations}, and an exogenous variable's future path has no innovation
to condition. Those scenarios instead pass a \strong{different \code{fit}} for the
scenario leg via \code{scenario_fit} -- typically the output of
\code{\link[=shock_exogenous_level]{shock_exogenous_level()}} -- with \code{restrictions = NULL}. The common
random-numbers argument still holds: \code{z_matrix}'s size depends only on
\code{horizon} and the number of endogenous variables, not on \code{ts_data}'s
content, so pairing works identically whether the two legs differ by a
restriction or by a different exogenous path.
